[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 1:14] A full bridge to the gap. Beyond what we've discussed, what else could Brew & Bean do to reverse this margin decline? Take a minute and give me as many ideas as you can.

[candidate silent 20s]

[candidate · 1:34] Let me go lever by lever.

On price, since nothing's been passed through in two years: a straight menu price increase, or a more surgical one on the most bean-intensive drinks. Coffee demand is fairly inelastic, and volume has been growing, which suggests room. Even a 5% price rise on $480M is $24M straight to the bottom line if volume holds.

On procurement: hedge or lock in forward contracts on beans, which they apparently haven't been doing; renegotiate with suppliers at 200-store scale; dual-source or shift origin; consider a slightly different bean blend.

On product mix: push higher-margin, low-bean items — food, teas, cold brew concentrate — and shift mix toward them.

On waste: check brew yield, shrinkage, and spoilage on dairy and food, since those are up 37.5%.

On packaging: cheaper cups, or incentivize reusable mugs.

On overhead, the 2-point creep is small but worth auditing — it's $9.6M.

And loyalty or subscription pricing to lock in volume if we raise prices.
